{"accession":"PSI-107","acronym":"SPICE","title":"Smoke Point In Co-flow Experiment","doi":"10.60555/9kbh-e962","psi_version":4,"license":"CC0-1.0","project_type":"Microgravity Investigation","flight_platform":"International Space Station (ISS)","hardware":"Microgravity Science Glovebox (MSG)","research_area":"Combustion Science","sub_research_area":"Fire Safety","nasa_center":"Glenn Research Center (GRC)","start_date":null,"end_date":null,"release_date":"18-Sep-2024","objective":"The objective of SPICE is to better understand the complex process of combustion by observing flames in a microgravity environment aboard the International Space Station (ISS) and analogous ground experiments.\n\nSpecific objectives were:\n(1) Identify the laminar smoke-point properties of non-buoyant jet diffusion flames under various co-flow conditions in microgravity.\n(2) Determine the effects of burner diameter and co-flow velocity on the luminous flame lengths and smoke points.\n(3) Quantify the sooting propensity of different fuels, such as ethylene, propane, and propylene mixtures.\n(4) Validate the correlation between luminous flame shape and residence time under microgravity conditions.\n(5) Investigate the impact of air and fuel flow rate variations on smoke point phenomena in space.","approach":"The experimental setup in the microgravity science glovebox consisted of an apparatus with a flow duct with integrated nozzles, cameras, and fuel delivery systems. Fuels tested included methane, ethylene, propane, propylene, and propylene mixtures. Each flame was ignited in a controlled co-flow air environment, with burner diameters of 0.41 mm, 0.76 mm, and 1.6 mm and co-flow velocities ranging from 5.4 cm/s to 65 cm/s. Fuel flow rates were adjusted to identify the smoke point, marked by the flame's transition to emitting soot. Data such as flame lengths were recorded using still and video cameras. Measurements included flame height, radiative heat losses, and co-flow air velocity distributions. In total 526 flames were studied.","hypothesis":"The research hypothesizes that by minimizing gravity-driven transport phenomena scientists can study the laminar smoke-point properties of non-buoyant jet diffusion flames. Flame properties are expected to differ significantly from those observed under normal gravity.","research_impacts":"(1) Will improve our understanding of soot formation mechanisms and help design safer spacecraft environments.\n(2) Will contribute to refining our computational models for predicting flame behaviors under non-buoyant conditions.\n(3) Will enhance our knowledge of soot generation\n(4) Could possibly provide insights to better fuel formulations and combustion processes.","related":[],"in_scope":true,"scope_note":null,"family":"MSG duct","fuel_phases":["gas"],"configuration":"Co-flow laminar jet diffusion flames","scale":"burner","pillars":["detection","prevention"],"needs":{"detection":0.6,"materials":0.2},"key_variables":["fuel","nozzle diameter","fuel flow","co-flow air velocity"],"sensors":["cameras (Nikon NEF)"],"uses_data_from":[],"summary":"Laminar smoke points and soot propensity of non-buoyant jet flames versus burner size and co-flow velocity (469 runs).","file_categories":{"Reports":{"n":3,"bytes":2990401,"examples":["PSI-107_Reports_SMOKE POINTS OF MICROGRAVITY AND  NORMAL GRAVITY COFLOW DIFFUSION  FLAMES_DotsonThesis.pdf","PSI-107_Reports_SPICE_30-DayPostFlightReport.pdf","PSI-107_Reports_SPICE_FinalReport.pdf"]},"Science Documents":{"n":1,"bytes":1112064,"examples":["PSI-107_Science Documents_SPICE_SRD.doc"]},"Presentations":{"n":5,"bytes":36912183,"examples":["PSI-107_Presentations_SPICE Overview.ppt","PSI-107_Presentations_Laminar Smoke Points in Coflow Measured Aboard ISS.pdf","PSI-107_Presentations_SPICE-1 Ops Overview and Smoke Point Cue Card.ppt","PSI-107_Presentations_IP-C Crew training 2010.pdf","PSI-107_Presentations_IP-D Crew training 2011.pdf"]},"Raw Data":{"n":172,"bytes":6899317356,"examples":["PSI-107_Raw Data_Ethylene assembled smoke hieght slide.  GMT 045 1503      11 26 2011.pptx","PSI-107_Raw Data_SPICE_GMT045_2009_NikonD100_PictureBook.ppt","PSI-107_Raw Data_Spice_Flight_Flamelength.xls","PSI-107_Raw Data_045_SPICE_DSC_num.txt","PSI-107_Raw Data_SmokePointReview-0g-3.xlsx","PSI-107_Raw Data_048_SPICE_num.txt"]},"Engineering Documents":{"n":4,"bytes":5431916,"examples":["PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_Phase III Flight Safety Data Package.pdf","PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE-MEM-010.pdf","PSI-107_Engineering Documents_60054MAFH000-C SPICE Experiment Assembly.pdf","PSI-107_Engineering Documents_SPICE_ScienceVerificationReport.pdf"]},"Reference Data":{"n":1,"bytes":1060135,"examples":["PSI-107_Reference Data_SPICE_VIDEO OVERLAY RESET.pdf"]}},"total_file_bytes":6946864573,"contacts":[{"name":"Peter Sunderland","affiliation":"University of Maryland","roles":["Co-Investigator (Co-I)"]},{"name":"David Urban","affiliation":"NASA GRC","roles":["Principal Investigator (PI)"]}],"psi_url":"https://psi.nasa.gov/physci/repo/data/investigations/PSI-107","profile":{"n_tests":469,"n_records":469,"record_types":{"test":469},"fuel_phases":["gas"],"ranges":{"flow_cm_s":[0.66,85.28]},"coverage":{"flow_cm_s":436},"measured_quantities":[{"quantity":"fuel_flow_mg_s","n":457},{"quantity":"fuel_flow_sccm","n":457},{"quantity":"nozzle_diameter","n":469}],"outcome_classes":{}},"publications":[{"title":"Laminar Smoke Points in Coflow Measured Aboard the International Space Station","authors":"Dotson Keenan, Sunderland Peter, Yuan Zeng-Guang, Urban David","doi":"10.2514/6.2010-1105"},{"title":"Scaling of Gas-Jet Flame Lengths in Elevated Gravity","authors":"Sunderland Peter B., Urban David L., Nayagam Vedha","doi":"10.1007/978-1-4020-8682-3_22"},{"title":"The Microgravity Science Glovebox (MSG), a Resource for Gravity-Dependent Phenomena Research on the International Space Station (ISS)","authors":"RA Spivey, LB Jeter, C Vonk","doi":""},{"title":"The Microgravity Science Glo